 This problem can be approached using the formula for the shortest distance between two skew lines. The distance between two skew lines given by \(\vec{r_1} = \vec{a_1} + t\vec{b_1}\) and \(\vec{r_2} = \vec{a_2} + s\vec{b_2}\) is given by the formula: \[
d = \frac{|(\vec{a_2} - \vec{a_1}) \cdot (\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2})|}{|\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2}|}
\] Here, \(\vec{a_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}\), \(\vec{b_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\), \(\vec{a_2} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\), and \(\vec{b_2} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}\). First, we find \(\vec{a_2} - \vec{a_1}\): \[
\vec{a_2} - \vec{a_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}
\] Next, we find the cross product \(\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2}\): \[
\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2} = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\ 2 & -2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 & -1 \end{vmatrix} = \hat{i}(2) - \hat{j}(-2 + 1) + \hat{k}(4 + 2) = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
\] The magnitude of \(\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2}\) is: \[
|\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2}| = \sqrt{2^2 + 1^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{4 + 1 + 36} = \sqrt{41}
\] Now, we find the dot product \((\vec{a_2} - \vec{a_1}) \cdot (\vec{b_1} \times \vec{b_2})\): \[
\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} = -3(2) + 1(1) + 2(6) = -6 + 1 + 12 = 7
\] Finally, the shortest distance is: \[
d = \frac{|7|}{\sqrt{41}} = \frac{7}{\sqrt{41}}
